## [§](#examples)Examples

A `Span` with `style` set to [`Style::default()`](../prelude/struct.Style.html#method.default "associated function ratatui::prelude::Style::default") can be created from a `&str`, a `String`, or any type convertible to [`Cow<str>`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/borrow/enum.Cow.html "enum alloc::borrow::Cow").

```
use ratatui_core::text::Span;

let span = Span::raw("test content");
let span = Span::raw(String::from("test content"));
let span = Span::from("test content");
let span = Span::from(String::from("test content"));
let span: Span = "test content".into();
let span: Span = String::from("test content").into();
```

Styled spans can be created using [`Span::styled`](../prelude/struct.Span.html#method.styled "associated function ratatui::prelude::Span::styled") or by converting strings using methods from the [`Stylize`](../prelude/trait.Stylize.html "trait ratatui::prelude::Stylize") trait.

```
use ratatui_core::style::{Style, Stylize};
use ratatui_core::text::Span;

let span = Span::styled("test content", Style::new().green());
let span = Span::styled(String::from("test content"), Style::new().green());

// using Stylize trait shortcuts
let span = "test content".green();
let span = String::from("test content").green();
```

`Span` implements the [`Styled`](../style/trait.Styled.html "trait ratatui::style::Styled") trait, which allows it to be styled using the shortcut methods defined in the [`Stylize`](../prelude/trait.Stylize.html "trait ratatui::prelude::Stylize") trait.

```
use ratatui_core::style::Stylize;
use ratatui_core::text::Span;

let span = Span::raw("test content").green().on_yellow().italic();
let span = Span::raw(String::from("test content"))
    .green()
    .on_yellow()
    .italic();
```

`Span` implements the [`Widget`](../prelude/trait.Widget.html "trait ratatui::prelude::Widget") trait, which allows it to be rendered to a [`Buffer`](../prelude/struct.Buffer.html "struct ratatui::prelude::Buffer"). Often apps will use the `Paragraph` widget instead of rendering `Span` directly, as it handles text wrapping and alignment for you.

